  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 210 AM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nSouthwest flow aloft will remain in place Thursday into Friday. This \nflow will continue to help draw sub-tropical moisture northeastward \ninto the region. ENS mean PWATS climb to above 1.5\" to the east of \nHighway 83 Thursday into Friday. This would be 150 to 200 percent of \nnormal and near the 95th to 99th percentile for this time of year. \nAs a subtle shortwave moves northeast toward the area, surface low \npressure will deepen across northeast Colorado. Southeast low-level \nflow will draw low-level moisture from the Gulf. This, combined with \nthe mid and upper level sub-tropical moisture, will bring the \npotential for locally heavy rainfall rates Thursday night along and \nto the north of a weak warm frontal boundary extending eastward from \nthe low. This front looks to reside somewhere near the Nebraska \nKansas border or slightly farther north nearer I-80. As the low-\nlevel jet strengthen  Thursday evening, numerous showers and \nthunderstorms should develop along and north of the front. As \nmentioned, locally heavy  rainfall will be of concern. from from the \nnorthern Rockies into the Dakotas Wednesday night, expect a cold \nfront to slowly move southeast across the area. This front should \nbecome the focus for at least scattered convective development. With \nsuch high moisture content, locally heavy rainfall rates will be \npossible. Showers appear to linger through much of the day Friday as \na very moist airmass remains in place. Ridging aloft then attempts \nto build northward into the area during the weekend, which would \nbring drier weather. \n\nTemperatures are expected to be below normal Thursday into Friday, \nwith highs in the upper 60s to lower 70s. Temperature moderation \noccurs as the ridging aloft builds into the region during the \nweekend, with highs near or above normal in the upper 70s to mid \n80s.",
